## 8. Possible Advantages Over Pure LLM Architectures

The architecture is intended to investigate whether certain classes of problems could be represented more naturally than through repeated matrix transformations.

Potential areas include:

### Deep hierarchical reasoning

Relationships may be explicitly represented as structures rather than implicitly embedded within vectors.

### Recursive reasoning

Recursive processes can remain compact objects instead of requiring immediate expansion.

### Long-horizon planning

Repeated or hierarchical plans may be represented as compositional structures.

Example:

```text
Plan(
    Repeat(SubPlan A, n),
    Condition B,
    RecursivePlan C
)
```

### Program and algorithm representation

Algorithms are naturally recursive and compositional, potentially making structured representations more appropriate than purely vector-based states.

### Mathematical reasoning

Symbolic relationships and recursive expressions could remain exact rather than being approximated exclusively through learned vector representations.

### World-model state spaces

The architecture could describe extremely large families of possible states while materializing only those relevant to the current reasoning process.

